Second, there must be a negative term in the sequence $\left\{ \xi
_{n}\right\} _{n\geq n_{1}+1}$. If not, namely, there is no negative term,
then $\xi _{n}>0$ for all $n\geq n_{1}+1$. Thus, $\xi _{n}>0$ for $n\in
\mathbb{N}$, and then, $g_{p_{0}^{\ast }}\left( x\right) =\sum_{n=0}^{\infty
}\xi _{n}x^{n}>0$ for all $x\in \left( 0,1\right) $, which, by (\ref{dfp}),
yields that $f_{p_{0}^{\ast }}\left( x\right) $ is increasing on $\left(
0,1\right) $. It leads to the same contradiction as (\ref{1>1}). Now assume
that $\xi _{n_{0}}$ ($n_{0}\geq n_{1}+1$) is the first negative term of the
sequence $\{\xi _{n}\}_{n\geq n_{1}+1}$, that is, $\xi _{n}\geq 0$ for $ 
n_{1}+1\leq n\leq n_{0}-1$ and $\xi _{n_{0}}<0$. We claim that all terms
after that ($n\geq n_{0}+1$) are negative, which indicates that $\left\{ \xi
_{n}\right\} _{n\geq 0}$ is a PN-type sequence. We now prove that $\xi
_{n}<0 $ for all $n\geq n_{0}+1\geq n_{1}+2$ by induction. Using the second
relation of (\ref{etan-rr+-}) yields 
\begin{equation*}
\eta _{n_{0}+1}=\xi _{n_{0}+1}-\theta _{n_{0}}\xi _{n_{0}}=\lambda
_{n_{0}}\left( \xi _{n_{0}}+\theta _{n_{0}-1}\left( -\xi _{n_{0}-1}\right)
\right) +\tau _{n_{0}}\gamma _{n_{0}}u_{n_{0}-1}<0,
\end{equation*} 
which gives that $\xi _{n_{0}+1}<\theta _{n_{0}}\xi _{n_{0}}<0$. Suppose
that $\xi _{k}<0$ for $n_{0}+1\leq k\leq n$. Using the second relation of ( 
\ref{etan-rr+-})\ again yields 
\begin{equation*}
\eta _{k+1}<\lambda _{k}\eta _{k}<\lambda _{k}\lambda _{k-1}\eta
_{k-1}<\cdot \cdot \cdot <\left( \lambda _{k}\lambda _{k-1}\cdot \cdot \cdot
\lambda _{n_{0}+1}\right) \eta _{n_{0}+1}<0,
\end{equation*} 
which leads to $\xi _{k+1}-\theta _{k}\xi _{k}<0$, and therefore, $\xi
_{k+1}<\theta _{k}\xi _{k}<0$. By induction we arrive at $\xi _{k}<0$ for
all $k\geq n_{0}$. Consequently, $\left\{ \xi _{n}\right\} _{n\geq 0}$ is a
PN-type sequence, and the assertion (\ref{A-gp0*-PN}) holds.

\textbf{Case 3}: $\delta \left( p_{0}^{\ast }\right) =0$, $\Delta \gamma
_{1}\left( p_{0}^{\ast }\right) <0$ and $\gamma _{1}\left( p_{0}^{\ast
}\right) \geq 0$.

\textbf{Case 3.1}: $\delta \left( p_{0}^{\ast }\right) =0$, $\Delta \gamma
_{1}\left( p_{0}^{\ast }\right) <0$ and $\gamma _{1}\left( p_{0}^{\ast
}\right) >0$. From Lemma \ref{L-sgngn-p} (ii) there is an $n_{1}\geq 1$ such
that $\gamma _{n}>0$ for $1\leq n\leq n_{1}-1$ and $\gamma _{n}<0$ for $ 
n\geq n_{1}+1$. Then the relation (\ref{etan-rr+-}) holds. By using the same
technique as Case 2, we can prove that $\left\{ \xi _{n}\right\} _{n\geq 0}$
is a PN-type sequence, and the assertion (\ref{A-gp0*-PN}) holds.

\textbf{Case 3.2}: $\delta \left( p_{0}^{\ast }\right) =0$, $\Delta \gamma
_{1}\left( p_{0}^{\ast }\right) <0$ and $\gamma _{1}\left( p_{0}^{\ast
}\right) =0$. By Lemma \ref{L-sgngn-p} (i) we see that $\gamma _{n}\leq 0$
for all $n\geq 1$. Using the same technique as Case 1, we can prove that $ 
\left\{ \xi _{n}\right\} _{n\geq 0}$ is a PN-type sequence, and the
assertion (\ref{A-gp0*-PN}) holds.

\textbf{Case 4}: $\delta \left( p_{0}^{\ast }\right) <0$, $\Delta \gamma
_{1}\left( p_{0}^{\ast }\right) >0$ and $\gamma _{1}\left( p_{0}^{\ast
}\right) \geq 0$. From Lemma \ref{L-sgngn-p} (ii) there is an $n_{1}\geq 1$
such that $\gamma _{n}>0$ for $1\leq n\leq n_{1}-1$ and $\gamma _{n}<0$ for $ 
n\geq n_{1}+1$. By using the same technique as Case 2, we can prove that $ 
\left\{ \xi _{n}\right\} _{n\geq 0}$ is a PN-type sequence, and the
assertion (\ref{A-gp0*-PN}) holds.

Finally, we prove that $p_{0}^{\ast }$ is the best possible. In fact,
solving the inequality 
\begin{equation*}
\lim_{x\rightarrow 1}\frac{\left[ F\left( -a,b;c;x\right) \right] ^{1/a}}{ 
M_{p}\left( 1-x,b/c\right) }\geq 1.
\end{equation*} 
for $p$ gives $p\leq p_{0}^{\ast }$; this together with the increasing
property of $p\mapsto M_{p}\left( 1-x,b/c\right) $ implies that $p_{0}^{\ast
}$ can not be replaced by any larger number than $p_{0}^{\ast }$. This
completes the proof.
